---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Best Running Watches for Treadmill Running (2026): 6 Picks";
const description =
  "Six current running watches checked for treadmill use: indoor run modes, post-run distance calibration, foot pod pairing, and GymKit or treadmill connections.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "coros-pace-4",
    name: "COROS PACE 4",
    category: "Best Overall Running Watch for Treadmill",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0FYGTNYRW?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$249",
    specs: "Indoor Run mode · post-run distance calibration · manual treadmill speed entry · COROS POD 2 and Stryd over Bluetooth · up to 19 days daily use",
    pros: [
      "COROS documents post-run distance calibration that also improves future indoor runs",
      "You can enter the treadmill speed before or during a run, useful for intervals",
      "Pairs with the COROS POD 2 or a Stryd foot pod over Bluetooth",
      "The lowest price of any watch here with all three of those features",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No GymKit or documented treadmill connection, and COROS’s Virtual Run broadcast to Zwift does not list the PACE 4",
      "The COROS POD 2 and a Stryd cannot be connected at the same time",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The best-equipped treadmill watch for the money. Buy it if you want to correct indoor distance easily and might add a foot pod later.",
    description: "The PACE 4 earns the top spot because COROS documents its treadmill features more clearly than anyone else. Its Indoor Run mode estimates pace from cadence, and COROS’s help centre says that at the end of an eligible run the watch prompts you to confirm the distance; entering the correct figure fixes that run and updates the model for future indoor runs. Corrections can be up to 25% either way on runs of at least 1 km, and do not apply if you used manual speed or a structured workout. You can also type in the treadmill speed and incline before or during a run. For better pace, it pairs with the COROS POD 2 or a Stryd over Bluetooth, though not both at once. COROS lists it at $249, which independent reviewers confirm, a $20 rise on the PACE 3, and quotes up to 19 days of daily use. COROS’s Zwift broadcast feature lists older models but not the PACE 4, so do not buy it expecting that.",
  },
  {
    id: "garmin-forerunner-170",
    name: "Garmin Forerunner 170",
    category: "Best Garmin Running Watch for Most Treadmill Runners",
    badge: "Most Runners",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Garmin+Forerunner+170&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Around $300",
    specs: "Treadmill running mode · ANT+ and Bluetooth foot pods · HRM pace and distance · up to 20 hr GPS-only",
    pros: [
      "Treadmill Running, Indoor Track and Virtual Run modes, according to Garmin and reviewers",
      "Pairs with foot pods over both ANT+ and Bluetooth",
      "Garmin lists running pace and distance from a compatible heart rate strap",
      "Adds Garmin Pay and cycling sensor support over the cheaper Forerunner 70",
    ],
    cons: [
      "We could not open this model’s manual to confirm the treadmill calibration step Garmin documents for its Forerunners",
      "No GymKit, and no documented treadmill connection",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The safe choice for most runners who want a Garmin. Save $50 with the Forerunner 70 if you will never use Garmin Pay or cycling sensors.",
    description: "Garmin launched the Forerunner 170 in May 2026, and it is the Forerunner we would point most treadmill runners to. Garmin’s spec page lists Treadmill Running, Indoor Track Running and Virtual Running modes, a foot pod capability and running pace and distance from a compatible heart rate monitor; an independent comparison confirms the indoor modes and says foot pods pair over ANT+ and Bluetooth. That combination matters, because it means almost any pod or Garmin strap can give it a better indoor pace than the wrist. Garmin’s Forerunner manuals describe a calibrate-and-save step after a treadmill run, but we could not open the manual for this model to confirm it, so check before relying on it. Garmin lists it at $299.99 and up to 20 hours in GPS-only mode, figures independent coverage agrees with. The cheaper Forerunner 70 has the same indoor modes and foot pod support at $249.99, while the 170 adds Garmin Pay, cycling power and smart trainer support.",
  },
  {
    id: "garmin-forerunner-970",
    name: "Garmin Forerunner 970",
    category: "Best Premium Running Watch for Treadmill Training",
    badge: "Premium Pick",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0F8QWPVNK?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Around $750",
    specs: "Treadmill running mode · foot pod capable · up to 26 hr GPS-only · up to 15 days smartwatch",
    pros: [
      "Garmin’s flagship Forerunner, with the full treadmill, indoor track and virtual run modes",
      "Extra running metrics, including step speed loss, with Garmin’s HRM 600 strap",
      "Foot pod capable and lists running pace and distance from a compatible heart rate strap",
      "Long battery: up to 15 days as a smartwatch and 26 hours GPS-only",
    ],
    cons: [
      "At about $750, a lot to pay for features that mostly matter outdoors",
      "Treadmill calibration step not confirmed from this model’s own manual, and no documented treadmill connection",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For runners who train seriously indoors and out and want Garmin’s most complete running watch. The Forerunner 170 does the treadmill basics for far less.",
    description: "The Forerunner 970 is Garmin’s top running watch, and on a treadmill it does what the 170 does, with more. Garmin’s spec page lists Treadmill Running, Indoor Track and Virtual Running modes, foot pod support, and running pace and distance from a compatible heart rate monitor; Garmin’s HRM 600 adds metrics such as step speed loss, which Garmin says the 970 supports. An independent review lists foot pods among its supported sensors. Garmin quotes up to 15 days as a smartwatch and up to 26 hours in GPS-only mode, and the same review reports the same figures. Garmin listed it at $749.99 on 15 October 2026, a figure the reviewer called a steep increase over its predecessor. As with the 170, Garmin’s Forerunner manuals describe a post-run treadmill calibration step but we could not open the 970’s manual to confirm it. Most of what you pay extra for — maps, the display, the speaker and microphone — is used outdoors, so buy it for the whole of your running, not for the treadmill.",
  },
  {
    id: "apple-watch-series-12",
    name: "Apple Watch Series 12",
    category: "Best for iPhone Users and Gym Treadmills",
    badge: "GymKit Pick",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Apple+Watch+Series+12&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "From $399",
    specs: "Indoor Run workout · GymKit gym equipment pairing · calibrates from outdoor GPS runs",
    pros: [
      "Pairs with GymKit treadmills and receives their workout data, according to Apple",
      "Indoor Run workout in the built-in Workout app",
      "Apple says Series 12 improves step count and distance measurement, including on a treadmill",
      "Works with the widest range of iPhone apps if you train with a third-party app",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No post-run distance entry in Apple’s documentation; calibration happens on outdoor GPS runs",
      "Apple documents no running foot pod pairing; Stryd works only through its own app",
      "GymKit treadmills are mostly found in gyms, not homes",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The right watch for iPhone owners, and the best on this page if you run on gym treadmills with GymKit. At home, expect to rely on outdoor calibration.",
    description: "Apple Watch is the only watch here that can receive data straight from a treadmill. Apple’s support guide says it pairs and syncs with compatible cardio equipment, including treadmills, for more accurate workout information, and that compatible machines carry a “Connects to Apple Watch” label; these are mostly commercial gym machines. Without GymKit, you record an Indoor Run in the Workout app and the watch estimates distance. Apple’s calibration works differently from the other watches here: its support page says to record outdoor walks or runs with good GPS, about 20 minutes at each pace you use, and that this improves accuracy when GPS is unavailable. There is no option in Apple’s documentation to type in the treadmill distance afterwards, and no documented foot pod pairing; Stryd supports Apple Watch through its own app. Apple says the Series 12 gives more accurate step count and distance, including on a treadmill, but publishes no figure. Apple lists the 42 mm model at $399, in line with independent coverage.",
  },
  {
    id: "polar-street-x",
    name: "Polar Street X",
    category: "Best Polar Watch for Treadmill Runners on a Budget",
    badge: "Budget Pick",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Polar+Street+X&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Around $250",
    specs: "Treadmill running profile · speed and distance from the wrist · Bluetooth stride sensor pairing and calibration",
    pros: [
      "Treadmill running profile with speed and distance from the wrist, per Polar’s manual",
      "Pairs with and calibrates Bluetooth stride sensors, such as a Stryd",
      "Can share heart rate with gym equipment and apps over Bluetooth",
      "An independent review says it runs the same software as the pricier Vantage M3 and Grit X2 Pro, apart from offline maps",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No post-run treadmill distance correction in Polar’s manual",
      "Bluetooth sensors only, and Polar no longer sells its own stride sensor in the US",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For runners who like Polar’s training tools and want them for around $250. Add a Stryd if treadmill pace matters.",
    description: "Polar’s newest watch is a lower-priced route into its full training software: an independent review says it runs the same software as the Vantage M3 and Grit X2 Pro, minus offline maps. On a treadmill, Polar’s user manual says the Treadmill running profile measures speed and distance from your wrist with the built-in accelerometer, and advises wearing the watch snugly and setting your height and handedness correctly. The manual also covers pairing a Bluetooth stride sensor and calibrating it, either by running a known distance of more than 400 metres or by entering a factor. What it does not describe is correcting a treadmill run’s distance after you finish, so if you want accurate indoor pace, pair a pod. The watch connects to Bluetooth sensors only. Polar listed it at $249.99 on 15 October 2026, matching independent coverage. The same review found its GPS inconsistent outdoors, which does not affect treadmill use but is worth knowing if you also run outside.",
  },
  {
    id: "suunto-run",
    name: "Suunto Run",
    category: "Best Lightweight Running Watch for Treadmill and Road",
    badge: "Simple Pick",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Suunto+Run+watch&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Around $249",
    specs: "Treadmill mode · foot POD support with automatic calibration · Bluetooth heart rate straps",
    pros: [
      "Treadmill running mode, according to Suunto and an independent review",
      "Suunto lists foot POD support with automatic calibration",
      "Pairs with Bluetooth heart rate straps",
      "One of the lowest prices for a current running watch from a major brand",
    ],
    cons: [
      "We could not confirm a post-run treadmill distance correction from Suunto’s documentation",
      "At launch it supported only heart rate straps and foot pods; other sensor types came later by update",
    ],
    bottomLine: "A good, light running watch for road and treadmill at a fair price. Pair a foot pod if you want dependable indoor pace.",
    description: "The Suunto Run is Suunto’s lightweight running watch, and it is still on sale at $249 alongside the newer, pricier Run 2. Suunto’s product page names track, treadmill and trail running modes, lists foot POD support with automatic calibration, and lists compatibility with Bluetooth heart rate straps. An independent review confirms the treadmill profile and the $249 price, and notes that at launch the watch paired only with heart rate straps and running foot pods, with power meters and cycling sensors added in a later update. We could not open Suunto’s user guide, and Suunto’s spec sheet does not list a way to correct a treadmill run’s distance afterwards, so we cannot tell you it has one; plan on a foot pod if indoor pace accuracy matters. The same review reported shorter battery life than Suunto’s larger watches. For a runner who wants a simple, light watch for the road with a usable treadmill mode, it is fair value.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Workouts & Training", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/training/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Running Watches for Treadmill", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-running-watches-for-treadmill/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best running watch for treadmill running?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most treadmill runners we would pick the COROS PACE 4, because COROS documents post-run distance calibration, manual treadmill speed entry and foot pod pairing for $249. Garmin's Forerunner 170 is the safe all-round choice, and Apple Watch Series 12 is best if your gym's treadmills support GymKit. Adding a foot pod improves indoor pace on any of them.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which watch is most accurate on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No maker publishes a treadmill accuracy figure for current watches, and independent reviewers mostly test accuracy outdoors. In practice, accuracy depends less on the watch than on how it gets your speed: a foot pod or a GymKit treadmill beats a wrist estimate, and calibrating against a belt speed you have checked beats calibrating against an unchecked console.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I correct my watch distance after a treadmill run?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On some watches. COROS documents a prompt at the end of eligible Indoor Runs, with corrections of up to 25% either way. Garmin's Forerunner manuals describe a calibrate-and-save step after a treadmill run. Apple Watch has no such option in Apple's documentation; it calibrates from outdoor GPS runs. We found no post-run correction in Polar's Street X manual.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does a Garmin watch connect to a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not for receiving the treadmill's speed, as far as Garmin's documentation for these models shows. Garmin's spec pages list smart trainer control, which is for indoor bike trainers. Garmin watches estimate treadmill pace themselves, or take it from a foot pod or a compatible heart rate strap such as the HRM 600. Zwift says Garmin watches can broadcast running speed to Zwift.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which watches work with GymKit treadmills?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "GymKit is Apple's system, so only Apple Watch uses it. Apple's support guide says the watch pairs and syncs with compatible cardio equipment, including treadmills marked \"Connects to Apple Watch\". These are mainly commercial gym treadmills. Garmin, COROS, Polar and Suunto watches do not use GymKit and rely on their own estimates or a sensor indoors.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a foot pod with a running watch on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not to record a run, since every watch here has a treadmill or indoor mode. A foot pod is worth adding if you want pace you can trust, especially for intervals, or if your watch has no post-run correction. COROS, Garmin, Polar and Suunto document foot pod pairing; Apple does not, though Stryd works through its own Apple Watch app.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-10-15",
      dateModified: "2026-10-15",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      offers: {
        "@type": "AggregateOffer",
        priceCurrency: "USD",
        availability: "https://schema.org/InStock",
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-15",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-15",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best running watch for treadmill",
      "best watch for treadmill running",
      "most accurate watch for treadmill",
      "treadmill calibration watch",
      "gymkit watch",
      "foot pod watch",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Our Top Picks", link: "#quick-picks" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-bestrunningwatchesfortreadmill" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-bestrunningwatchesfortreadmill)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buying Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Running Watches <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">for the Treadmill</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Every running watch has a treadmill mode. What separates them indoors is whether you can correct the distance after a run, whether they pair with a foot pod, and whether they can talk to the treadmill at all. Here are six current models, checked feature by feature against their makers’ own documentation.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#quick-picks" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            See the Picks
          </a>
          <a href="#treadmill-features" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Compare Treadmill Features
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 15, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="2.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the products below.</strong> We have not tested these
          watches. On 15 October 2026 we confirmed each current model name and price on its
          maker's own store (Garmin, Apple, COROS, Polar and Suunto) and checked each treadmill
          feature against the maker's own product page, help centre or user manual, then
          cross-checked prices and specifications against independent review coverage. A figure
          appears in a card's specification line only where those sources agree; where they
          disagreed, or where we could only find one source, we left the figure out and say so.
          No maker publishes a treadmill accuracy percentage for these watches, so this page
          quotes none. None of these figures are our own measurements. Prices change often and
          features change with software updates, so confirm the price and the feature on the
          exact model before you order. We earn a commission if you buy through the Amazon links
          on this page; it does not change which products we list or what we say about them.
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What this page is, and is not.</strong> We review
          equipment; this page is not medical or coaching advice. Heart rate and training figures
          from a watch are estimates, and anyone with a heart condition, an injury or a concern
          about exercise should talk to a qualified professional before relying on them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#quick-picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Our Picks at a Glance</a>
          <a href="#picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The 6 Best Running Watches for Treadmill Running</a>
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. How We Chose These Watches</a>
          <a href="#treadmill-features" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. What a Watch Can and Cannot Do on a Treadmill</a>
          <a href="#calibration" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Post-Run Calibration: Which Watches Let You Fix the Distance</a>
          <a href="#foot-pods-and-straps" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Foot Pods and Chest Straps: The Accuracy Upgrade</a>
          <a href="#gymkit-ftms" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. GymKit and FTMS: Connecting a Watch to the Treadmill</a>
          <a href="#set-up" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Setting Up Any Watch for Treadmill Runs</a>
          <a href="#heart-rate" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Heart Rate on the Treadmill</a>
          <a href="#who-should-skip" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Who Should Buy Something Else</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Indoors, a running watch loses the thing it is best at. With no GPS signal, it
          estimates your pace from how your arm and wrist move, and the treadmill console and the
          watch rarely agree. The good news is that some watches handle this far better than
          others, and the differences are documented, not guesswork. This page compares six current
          watches on the four features that matter on a treadmill, and explains which of them will
          give you a believable number without buying anything else.
        </p>

        <div id="quick-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Our Picks at a Glance</h2>
<p>
            Six watches, chosen for what they do on a treadmill rather than on a mountain. The short
            version: the COROS PACE 4 has the best-documented treadmill features at its price, the
            Garmin Forerunner 170 is the safest choice for most runners, and the Apple Watch Series
            12 is the one to wear if you run on gym treadmills that support GymKit. Want to check
            the treadmill before you blame the watch? Start with our
            <a href="/treadmill-calibration/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calibration guide</a>.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Pick</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Best for</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Why</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">COROS PACE 4</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Best overall for treadmill runners</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Documented post-run distance correction, manual treadmill speed entry, foot pod support, $249</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Garmin Forerunner 170</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Most runners</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill mode, ANT+ and Bluetooth foot pods, Garmin's wider ecosystem, about $300</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Garmin Forerunner 970</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Serious training</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Flagship Forerunner with extra running metrics from the HRM 600 strap</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Apple Watch Series 12</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">iPhone users and gym treadmills</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The only pick that pairs with GymKit treadmills</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Polar Street X</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Polar fans on a budget</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill profile, Bluetooth stride sensor calibration, around $250</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Suunto Run</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">A light, simple running watch</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill mode and foot POD support for $249</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            If accuracy is the reason you are shopping, read
            <a href="#foot-pods-and-straps" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">foot pods and chest straps</a>
            before you choose. A $100 to $300 sensor improves treadmill pace on any of these watches
            more than moving up a model does.
          </p>
        </div>

