              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Treadmill mode</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Post-run calibration</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Foot pod</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">GymKit / FTMS</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">COROS PACE 4</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes (Indoor Run)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes, documented</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes (Bluetooth)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not documented</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Garmin Forerunner 170</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">In Garmin manuals; model manual not checked</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes (ANT+ and Bluetooth)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not documented for treadmills</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Garmin Forerunner 970</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">In Garmin manuals; model manual not checked</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not documented for treadmills</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Apple Watch Series 12</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes (Indoor Run)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No; calibrates from outdoor GPS runs</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not in Apple's documentation</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">GymKit, yes</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Polar Street X</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes (Treadmill running)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not in the manual</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes (Bluetooth)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not documented</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Suunto Run</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not confirmed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not documented</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            "Not documented" means we could not find the feature in the maker's own documentation
            for that model; it does not mean a future update could not add it. Two things stand
            out. First, GymKit is Apple-only, and no other maker here documents receiving speed
            from a treadmill over Bluetooth FTMS. In practice that means every non-Apple watch on
            this page relies on its own estimate or on a sensor indoors. Second, the watches differ
            most on calibration, which is where the next section starts.
          </p>
          <p>
            As for "most accurate": no maker publishes a treadmill accuracy figure for these
            watches, and independent reviewers mostly test accuracy outdoors. The honest answer is
            that the most accurate treadmill set-up is any of these watches paired with a foot pod,
            calibrated against a belt speed you have checked. The watch matters less than the
            sensor and the check.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calibration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Post-Run Calibration: Which Watches Let You Fix the Distance</h2>
<p>
            Post-run calibration means finishing a treadmill run, telling the watch the real
            distance, and having it both correct that run and learn from it. It is the single most
            useful treadmill feature a watch can have, because wrist estimates drift with pace,
            arm carriage and fatigue.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">COROS: the clearest documentation</h3>
          <p>
            COROS's help centre describes it in detail. At the end of an eligible Indoor Run the
            watch prompts you to confirm the total distance, and entering a corrected figure updates
            its cadence-pace model for future indoor runs. COROS sets limits: the correction can be
            up to 25% either way, the run must be at least 1 km, and it does not apply when you used
            manual speed or a structured workout. COROS also lets you type in the treadmill's speed
            before or during a run, which it suggests for interval sessions where pace changes
            often.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Garmin: calibrate and save</h3>
          <p>
            Garmin's Forerunner manuals describe running at least a mile on the treadmill, then
            choosing to calibrate when you save and entering the distance from the treadmill
            display; after the first calibration, a Calibrate and Save option lets you repeat it,
            and Garmin advises recalibrating on a different treadmill. We confirmed this in a
            Garmin Forerunner manual but could not open the manuals for the 170 or 970 themselves,
            so check the current manual for your model.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Apple: learn outdoors, apply indoors</h3>
          <p>
            Apple's support page says the watch calibrates by recording outdoor walks or runs with
            good GPS, ideally about 20 minutes at each pace you use, and that this improves accuracy
            when GPS is limited or unavailable. There is no option in Apple's documentation to type
            in the treadmill's distance after an Indoor Run. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-connect-apple-watch-to-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">connecting an Apple Watch to a treadmill</a>
            covers the workarounds.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Polar and Suunto</h3>
          <p>
            Polar's Street X manual describes speed and distance from the wrist in its Treadmill
            running profile and explains how to calibrate a stride sensor, but we found no option to
            correct a treadmill run's distance afterwards. For the Suunto Run, Suunto's spec sheet
            lists automatic foot POD calibration, but we could not confirm a post-run treadmill
            correction from Suunto's own documentation. If correcting distance matters to you, a
            foot pod is the route on both.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever watch you use, calibrate it against a distance you trust. Teaching a watch to
            agree with a console that reads fast just makes the watch read fast too; our guide on
            <a href="/is-my-treadmill-speed-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether your treadmill speed is accurate</a>
            shows why that happens.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="foot-pods-and-straps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Foot Pods and Chest Straps: The Accuracy Upgrade</h2>
<p>
            The biggest improvement you can make to treadmill pace is not a more expensive watch.
            It is a sensor that measures your feet, or your torso, instead of your wrist.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A foot pod</strong> clips to your shoe and reports pace and distance. Every
            watch here except the Apple Watch documents foot pod pairing. COROS watches pair with the
            COROS POD 2 or a Stryd over Bluetooth; Garmin's spec pages list the Forerunner 170 and
            970 as foot pod capable, and reviewers report both ANT+ and Bluetooth pods work; Polar's
            manual covers pairing and calibrating Bluetooth stride sensors; and Suunto lists foot
            POD support for the Run. Our roundup of the
            <a href="/best-foot-pods-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best foot pods for treadmill running</a>
            says which pods still on sale pair with which watch brands — several popular pods have
            been discontinued.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A chest strap</strong> is usually bought for heart rate, but some do more.
            Garmin's spec pages for the Forerunner 170 and 970 list running pace and distance from a
            compatible heart rate monitor, and Garmin says its HRM 600 strap sends pace and distance
            from treadmill runs to compatible watches. For a Garmin runner, that can replace a foot
            pod. On other brands, a strap improves heart rate, not pace.
          </p>
          <p>
            For Apple Watch owners, the options are narrower. Stryd supports Apple Watch through its
            own watch app, and on a GymKit treadmill the machine itself supplies the data. Otherwise
            the watch's own estimate, calibrated outdoors, is what you get.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gymkit-ftms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. GymKit and FTMS: Connecting a Watch to the Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            The tidiest solution would be for the treadmill to tell the watch its speed. Two
            standards could do that, and only one of them reaches a watch on this page.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Apple GymKit.</strong> Apple's support guide says Apple Watch can pair and sync
            data with compatible cardio equipment, including treadmills, for more accurate workout
            information, and that compatible machines carry a "Connects to Apple Watch" label. In
            practice that label appears mostly on commercial gym treadmills, not home machines. If
            you run at a gym with GymKit treadmills, an Apple Watch gets the treadmill's distance
            with no calibration at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bluetooth FTMS.</strong> FTMS is the open fitness-machine standard many
            connected home treadmills use to send speed to apps such as Zwift. None of the watch
            makers here documents receiving treadmill speed over FTMS on these models. Garmin's
            spec pages list smart trainer control, which is aimed at indoor cycling trainers rather
            than treadmills. So an FTMS treadmill helps your app, not your watch.
          </p>
          <p>
            Watches can still send data the other way. Polar's manual describes sharing heart rate
            with gym equipment and apps over Bluetooth, and Zwift's hardware page says Garmin
            watches that broadcast running speed, cadence and heart rate can be used with Zwift on
            compatible treadmills. If Zwift is part of your plan, our
            <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift treadmill guide</a>
            covers which signal should come from where.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="set-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Setting Up Any Watch for Treadmill Runs</h2>
<p>
            The watch you buy matters less than how you set it up. These steps apply to every
            model on this page, with the brand-specific details noted.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">1. Use the treadmill mode, every time</h3>
          <p>
            Start the treadmill or indoor run profile, not the outdoor one. An outdoor profile
            will look for GPS, may record a drifting track from the satellites it can see through a
            window, and will not use the indoor pace model. It also keeps treadmill runs separate
            in your history, which matters when you compare paces later.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">2. Get the personal settings right</h3>
          <p>
            Wrist-based pace depends on what the watch knows about you. Polar's manual, for
            example, asks for correct height and handedness, a snug fit and a consistent position
            on the wrist. Set your height and the wrist you wear it on in every brand's app; a
            watch set up for someone else will be wrong from the first step.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">3. Calibrate early, at more than one pace</h3>
          <p>
            Whatever calibration your watch offers, do it in the first few weeks and at the paces
            you actually run. COROS and Garmin learn from post-run corrections; Apple asks for
            outdoor GPS runs of about 20 minutes at each pace. One easy run will not teach a watch
            your interval pace. And check the treadmill's real belt speed before you enter its
            distance into anything.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">4. Pair sensors before the warm-up</h3>
          <p>
            If you use a foot pod or chest strap, wake it and wait for the watch to show it as
            connected before you press start. COROS's help centre, for instance, says to wait until
            the accessory icon turns solid. A sensor that connects halfway through leaves the first
            part of the run on the wrist estimate.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">5. Use the manual speed option for intervals</h3>
          <p>
            On a COROS watch, entering the treadmill's speed directly, and updating it when you
            change pace, sidesteps the estimate altogether. It is the most accurate option for
            interval sessions where the pace changes every minute, provided the treadmill's speed
            is right. On other watches, a foot pod does the same job more automatically.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">6. Pick one source of truth</h3>
          <p>
            The console, the watch, the pod and the app will each show a slightly different number.
            Decide which you trust — ideally a calibrated pod or a checked belt — and use it
            consistently, so this month's runs compare fairly with last month's.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heart-rate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Heart Rate on the Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Every watch here measures heart rate at the wrist, and indoors that reading has fewer
            obstacles than outdoors: no cold, no bouncing on rough ground. Wrist sensors still tend
            to lag at the start of hard intervals, which matters more on a treadmill, where interval
            sessions are common.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you train by heart rate zones, a chest strap paired to the watch is the more
            dependable source for interval work, and our
            <a href="/chest-strap-vs-wrist-heart-rate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">chest strap versus wrist heart rate</a>
            comparison explains the trade-offs. Our picks of the
            <a href="/best-heart-rate-monitors-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best heart rate monitors for treadmill training</a>
            cover straps that can talk to a watch and an app at the same time.
          </p>
          <p>
            Getting heart rate from the watch to a treadmill console or an app is a separate
            question. Garmin's spec pages list heart rate broadcast over ANT+ to paired devices,
            Polar's manual describes sharing heart rate with apps and gym equipment over Bluetooth,
            and COROS says its watches can pair an external Bluetooth strap for more precision. If
            you want heart rate on the console and the watch at once, a strap that connects to
            several devices is usually simpler than relying on the watch to pass it on.
          </p>
          <p>
            Zones themselves are explained in our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-zones/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill heart rate zones</a>.
            Treat the zones a watch sets for you as a starting estimate, not a prescription, and
            follow medical advice over any number on a screen if you have been given a limit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            A running watch is the right buy for anyone who runs both indoors and outdoors and
            wants one record of all of it. It is the wrong buy in a few common cases.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you only ever run on one treadmill,</strong> you may not need a watch for
            pace at all. The console, checked once against a stopwatch, is your pace source; a
            chest strap gives you heart rate, and a foot pod or treadmill sensor gets data into an
            app. Spend the difference on the treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you mostly walk on a treadmill or walking pad,</strong> a running watch is
            more than you need. Step counts and walking distance indoors are covered in our guide to
            <a href="/does-a-fitness-tracker-count-steps-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether a fitness tracker counts steps on a treadmill</a>,
            and a fitness tracker is usually the better buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you already own a recent running watch,</strong> do not replace it for
            treadmill accuracy. Add a foot pod, or calibrate the watch properly, and you will gain
            more than an upgrade would give you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want the treadmill to drive a training app,</strong> the watch is not
            the important purchase. Look at treadmills that broadcast over FTMS, or at our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apple-watch/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for Apple Watch</a>
            if GymKit is what you are after.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best running watch for treadmill running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most treadmill runners we would pick the COROS PACE 4, because COROS documents post-run distance calibration, manual treadmill speed entry and foot pod pairing for $249. Garmin's Forerunner 170 is the safe all-round choice, and Apple Watch Series 12 is best if your gym's treadmills support GymKit. Adding a foot pod improves indoor pace on any of them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which watch is most accurate on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No maker publishes a treadmill accuracy figure for current watches, and independent reviewers mostly test accuracy outdoors. In practice, accuracy depends less on the watch than on how it gets your speed: a foot pod or a GymKit treadmill beats a wrist estimate, and calibrating against a belt speed you have checked beats calibrating against an unchecked console.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I correct my watch distance after a treadmill run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On some watches. COROS documents a prompt at the end of eligible Indoor Runs, with corrections of up to 25% either way. Garmin's Forerunner manuals describe a calibrate-and-save step after a treadmill run. Apple Watch has no such option in Apple's documentation; it calibrates from outdoor GPS runs. We found no post-run correction in Polar's Street X manual.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a Garmin watch connect to a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for receiving the treadmill's speed, as far as Garmin's documentation for these models shows. Garmin's spec pages list smart trainer control, which is for indoor bike trainers. Garmin watches estimate treadmill pace themselves, or take it from a foot pod or a compatible heart rate strap such as the HRM 600. Zwift says Garmin watches can broadcast running speed to Zwift.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which watches work with GymKit treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                GymKit is Apple's system, so only Apple Watch uses it. Apple's support guide says the watch pairs and syncs with compatible cardio equipment, including treadmills marked "Connects to Apple Watch". These are mainly commercial gym treadmills. Garmin, COROS, Polar and Suunto watches do not use GymKit and rely on their own estimates or a sensor indoors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a foot pod with a running watch on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not to record a run, since every watch here has a treadmill or indoor mode. A foot pod is worth adding if you want pace you can trust, especially for intervals, or if your watch has no post-run correction. COROS, Garmin, Polar and Suunto document foot pod pairing; Apple does not, though Stryd works through its own Apple Watch app.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Choosing a Watch for Treadmill Running</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most treadmill frustration with watches comes from expecting the wrist to do a job it is not suited to. These four are the most common.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Upgrading the watch to fix indoor pace</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A more expensive watch still estimates treadmill pace from your wrist and cadence. If indoor pace is the problem, a foot pod, a compatible heart rate strap on Garmin, or proper calibration will do more than a newer model. Spend on the sensor first, and upgrade the watch only for features you will use outdoors as well.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Calibrating against an unchecked console</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Post-run calibration teaches the watch to agree with whatever distance you enter. If the treadmill console reads fast, the watch learns to read fast too, and every future run inherits the error. Check the belt speed with a mark and a stopwatch first, then enter that distance when the watch asks.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Bluetooth means the treadmill talks to the watch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill with Bluetooth usually sends data to its own app, or to Zwift over FTMS. None of the non-Apple watches here documents receiving treadmill speed, and GymKit is mostly on gym treadmills. Do not buy a treadmill or a watch expecting them to sync unless the maker names that pairing for your exact model.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Holding the rails or a phone on the watch arm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Wrist-based treadmill pace depends on natural arm swing. Polar’s manual, for example, advises wearing the watch snugly and not holding anything in that hand. Gripping the handrails or reading a phone with the watch arm can make recorded distance far too low. Swing your arms normally, or use a foot pod.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

